    201→## Settings
    …
    203→The settings object is the core of microbootstrap.
    205→All framework-related settings inherit from the `BaseServiceSettings` object. `BaseServiceSettings` defines parameters for the service and various instruments.
    207→However, the number of parameters is <b>not confined</b> to those defined in `BaseServiceSettings`. You can add as many as you need.
    209→These parameters can be sourced from your environment. By default, no prefix is added to these parameters.
    211→Example:
    213→```python
    214→class YourSettings(BaseServiceSettings):
    215→    service_debug: bool = True
    216→    service_name: str = "micro-service"
    218→    your_awesome_parameter: str = "really awesome"
    220→    ... # Other settings here
    221→```
    223→To source `your_awesome_parameter` from the environment, set the environment variable named `YOUR_AWESOME_PARAMETER`.
    225→If you prefer to use a prefix when sourcing parameters, set the `ENVIRONMENT_PREFIX` environment variable in advance.
    227→Example:
    229→```bash
    230→$ export ENVIRONMENT_PREFIX=YOUR_PREFIX_
    231→```
    233→Then the settings object will attempt to source the variable named `YOUR_PREFIX_YOUR_AWESOME_PARAMETER`.
    235→## Service settings
    …
    237→Each settings object for every framework includes service parameters that can be utilized by various instruments.
    239→You can configure them manually, or set the corresponding environment variables and let <b>microbootstrap</b> to source them automatically.
    241→```python
    242→from microbootstrap.settings import BaseServiceSettings
    245→class ServiceSettings(BaseServiceSettings):
    246→    service_debug: bool = True
    247→    service_environment: str | None = None
    248→    service_name: str = "micro-service"
    249→    service_description: str = "Micro service description"
    250→    service_version: str = "1.0.0"
    252→    ... # Other settings here
    254→```
    256→## Instruments
    …
    258→At present, the following instruments are supported for bootstrapping:
    260→- `sentry`
    261→- `prometheus`
    262→- `opentelemetry`
    263→- `pyroscope`
    264→- `logging`
    265→- `cors`
    266→- `swagger`
    268→Let's clarify the process required to bootstrap these instruments.
    270→### [Sentry](https://sentry.io/)
    …
    312→### [Prometheus](https://prometheus.io/)
    …
    394→### [OpenTelemetry](https://opentelemetry.io/)
    …
    451→### [Pyroscope](https://pyroscope.io)
    …
    453→To integrate Pyroscope, specify the `pyroscope_endpoint`.
    455→- The `opentelemetry_service_name` will be used as the application name.
    456→- `service_namespace` tag will be added with `opentelemetry_namespace` value.
    457→- You can also set `pyroscope_sample_rate`, `pyroscope_auth_token`, `pyroscope_tags` and `pyroscope_additional_params` — params that will be passed to `pyroscope.configure`.
    459→When both Pyroscope and OpenTelemetry are enabled, profile span IDs will be included in traces using [`pyroscope-otel`](https://github.com/grafana/otel-profiling-python) for correlation.
    461→Note that Pyroscope integration is not supported on Windows.
